[ROCm] Preserve amdsmi library selection during import (#197482)

## Summary

During ROCm initialization, PyTorch temporarily intercepts `ctypes.CDLL` calls made by `amdsmi`.

This change:

- routes unversioned `libamd_smi.so` requests, including explicit paths, through the existing compatibility candidates before falling back to the originally requested path;
- preserves the `$ROCM_HOME`-first order when the requested path is itself `$ROCM_HOME/lib/libamd_smi.so`;
- leaves versioned paths such as `libamd_smi.so.27` unchanged so the wrapper retains its ABI-selected library;
- preserves loader-path lookup before the packaged `_rocm_sdk_core` fallback;
- treats only `undefined symbol:` `AttributeError`s as an unavailable AMD SMI dependency while propagating unrelated `AttributeError`s.

## Test plan

- Python lint checks passed for the modified files.
- Added ROCm subprocess coverage for:
  - preserving versioned library paths even when an unversioned fallback exists;
  - redirecting explicit unversioned paths;
  - preferring loader-path resolution over the packaged fallback;
  - handling `undefined symbol:` failures without aborting `import torch`;
  - propagating unrelated `AttributeError`s.
- Replaced the nondeterministic first mapped shared library test fixture with the loaded `libc.so.*`.
- Latest GitHub ROCm CI is running on the exported commit.

Pull Request resolved: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/197482
Approved by: https://github.com/jeffdaily

[MPS] prod to metal (#198688)

Port prod to the shared reduction kernels. Only change that needs explanation I think is change in `reduction_utils.h`. See reproducer of why that change is needed here:
```python
import torch

# Second stage of c10::metal::threadgroup_prod<long>. pad=0 is the old code
# (only nsimd lanes active), pad=1 the fix (whole simdgroup, identity-padded).
lib = torch.mps.compile_shader("""
#include <c10/metal/reduction_utils.h>

kernel void tg_prod(
    device long* out,
    constant long& pad,
    uint idx [[thread_position_in_threadgroup]],
    uint size [[threads_per_threadgroup]]) {
  threadgroup long data[32];
  auto rc = c10::metal::simd_prod(idx < 2 ? 3L : 1L);
  if (idx % 32 == 0) {
    data[idx / 32] = rc;
  }
  metal::threadgroup_barrier(metal::mem_flags::mem_threadgroup);
  const uint nsimd = (size + 31) / 32;
  if (idx < (pad ? 32 : nsimd)) {
    auto rc1 = c10::metal::simd_prod(idx < nsimd ? data[idx] : 1L);
    if (idx == 0) {
      out[0] = rc1;
    }
  }
}
""")

out = torch.empty(1, dtype=torch.long, device="mps")

def run(threads, pad):
    lib.tg_prod(out, pad, threads=threads, group_size=threads)
    return out.item()

for threads in (64, 256, 1024):
    print(f"{threads:4d} threads: old={run(threads, 0)} fixed={run(threads, 1)} expected=9")

```

Gave:
```
64 threads: old=0 fixed=9 expected=9
 256 threads: old=0 fixed=9 expected=9
1024 threads: old=9 fixed=9 expected=9
```

Als notably, before int32 prod was accumulating in int32 which overflowed easily, now it does it in int64. Added test tests that exact behaviour.

Perf:

| Op | bf16 before (us) | bf16 after (us) | bf16 before/after | f32 before/after | int32 before/after |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `[4096, 4096]` | 141 | 119 | 1.18x | 1.14x | 1.05x |
| `[4096, 4096]`, dim=-1 | 125 | 123 | 1.02x | 1.06x | 0.99x |
| `[4096, 4096]`, dim=0 | 180 | 142 | 1.26x | 1.09x | 1.10x |
| `[8192, 1024]`, dim=0 | 66.5 | 39.7 | 1.67x | 1.09x | 1.18x |
| `[64, 1024, 64]`, dim=1 | 48.9 | 20.7 | 2.36x | 1.55x | 1.57x |
| `[16, 1000000]`, dim=-1 | 125 | 110 | 1.14x | 1.01x | 1.04x |
| `[1000000, 4]`, dim=0 | 38.7 | 27.8 | 1.39x | 1.17x | 1.10x |
| `[1000000]` | 23.7 | 8.7 | 2.73x | 2.34x | 1.69x |
| `[256, 1000]`, dim=1 | 17.9 | 4.9 | 3.63x | 3.36x | 3.13x |
| `[1024]` | 16.1 | 4.4 | 3.69x | 3.30x | 3.21x |

